{{fbdoc item="title" value="Supporting tools and tests"}}

The implementation trees rely on declarations, target wrappers, Make policy and regression harnesses maintained elsewhere in the repository. These trees answer different questions: how a program calls an API, how it is packaged, which sources enter its libraries, and how its behavior is checked.

{{fbdoc item="section" value="Target wrappers and application bridges"}}

##src/tools## contains tools shipped or used with particular targets. These tools coordinate SDKs and application lifecycle details outside the compiler's language implementation.

	- ##src/tools/android##: ##fbc-android## coordinates native compilation and APK packaging; Java input/activity classes and ##fb_android_app.c## bridge the Android application environment
	- ##src/tools/js##: ##fbc-js-app## chooses browser output and stages assets through the Emscripten wrapper
	- ##src/tools/wii##: ##fbc-wii## supplies devkitPro/libogc tools and ELF/DOL packaging
	- ##src/tools/wince##: Windows CE compiler wrappers and architecture-specific invocation defaults
	- ##src/tools/win32##: The ##fbc-win32## wrapper
	- ##src/tools/xbox##: ##fbc-xbox-xiso## packages an Xbox application image
	- ##src/tools/riscos##: Native application ##!Run##, ##!Boot## and ##!Help## integration

A wrapper can select a compiler and SDK or construct an application package. The compiler still owns BASIC parsing and code generation. Native services remain in the runtime libraries. When tracing Android input, for example, follow the Java/native bridge and the selected runtime driver as well as the packaging script.

##build_scripts## contains developer automation, source validators, packaging and target qualification runners. Start at the script called by the relevant Make target instead of running every similarly named script. The root ##tools## directory also contains local probes and automation; it is separate from the target helper tree under ##src/tools##.

{{fbdoc item="section" value="BASIC declarations and examples"}}

##inc## supplies BASIC-facing declarations for runtime interfaces, native APIs and external libraries. A ##.bi## file describes how compiled code calls an implementation; it does not necessarily supply that implementation. Read its calling convention, type widths and structure layout alongside the runtime or external API it describes.

##examples## demonstrates language and library use. The ##examples/manual## tree supplies compact language examples, while graphics, sound and platform directories demonstrate larger APIs. Some examples need optional libraries or SDKs. An example is useful as a reproduction, but a permanent regression should assert the specific behavior in the test suite.

{{fbdoc item="section" value="Choose the test that owns the failure"}}

The main compiler/runtime suite is ##tests##, with the harness described in ##tests/readme.txt##. The ##.ss## fixtures under ##src/tests/tmp## are a separate data tree and are not the main language test entry point.

	- Accepted/rejected language form or emitted diagnostic: Topic directory under ##tests##, using the appropriate ##TEST_MODE##
	- Runtime result, lifetime or descriptor behavior: Topic tests built through ##tests/unit-tests.mk##, or a focused native/runtime runner
	- Multi-module or C interoperability behavior: A ##.bmk## fixture consumed by ##tests/bmk-make.mk##
	- Software graphics or queued-renderer behavior: ##tests/gfx## and ##tests/gfx3##
	- Sound state, timing or captured samples: ##tests/sfx##
	- Source ownership, includes or selected archive members: ##check-compiler-structure.py##, ##check-rtlib-structure.py## or ##check-gfx-source-graph.py## under ##build_scripts##
	- Build ordering, tool failures, packaging or cleanup: ##mk/tests## and ##build_scripts/test-make-contracts.py##

##tests/log-tests.mk## handles compile-only, compile-and-run and multi-module expectations. Its saved records distinguish expected compiler rejection from abnormal failure or timeout. The log-test guide (##docs/compiler-test-harness.md##) explains retry selections and artifact ownership.

##make warning-tests## generates reports in disposable storage and compares all five target profiles with ##tests/warnings/r##. Differences fail without changing reference files. Use ##make warning-tests-update## only for an intentional diagnostic change, then review the reference diff. ##make gfx-locking-test## checks input callbacks, temporary allocation, allocation failure and repeated shutdown with native MT libraries; the contract is explained in ##docs/gfx-locking.md##.

{{fbdoc item="section" value="Build ownership and generated files"}}

The root ##GNUmakefile## composes the layers under ##mk##. Platform identity and feature policy precede source selection; compile and archive rules consume the resulting lists. Installation and distribution consume completed artifacts. The Make file map (##mk/File-Map.md##) identifies the owner of each decision.

Use ##make print-config## to inspect the effective configuration. Structural entry points include ##make compiler-structure-test##, ##make gfx-structure-test##, and ##make make-contracts-test##. The runtime structure checker is ##build_scripts/check-rtlib-structure.py##. These checks validate ownership and build contracts; behavioral changes still need the corresponding executable regression.

Object trees, emitted bootstrap sources, linked libraries and test logs are generated inputs or outputs. Do not edit them to repair the maintained source. Quick/full build workflows include cleanup and bootstrap/package operations, so use one workflow per owned worktree. Normal and bootstrap compiler builds track effective commands, flags and selected tool contents. Independent compiler/library builds can use separate ##BUILD_ROOT=build/name## output roots. Follow the Make guide (##mk/README.md##) for record ownership. ##make source-artifacts-test## rejects generated ##.dump## and ##.ctu-info## files under maintained source directories and fails if a directory cannot be scanned; put analysis output in build storage.

##doc## contains the generated manual's source cache and tools as well as API notes. docs (##docs/README.md##) contains maintainer and platform guides. Keep language syntax documentation, implementation explanations and validation records linked to each other, with each retaining its own purpose.

##make source-docs-test## checks source-guide links and native navigation. ##make source-docs-render-test FBDOC=/path/to/fbdoc## also generates fresh HTML, fbhelp and text in disposable storage and checks the resulting pages.


